WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.969
Alerts should point to conditions that require action, not every interesting fluctuation.

00:00:04.969 --> 00:00:10.650
In AI environments, noisy alerts can be especially tempting because there are many signals:

00:00:10.650 --> 00:00:15.113
latency, queue depth, GPU pressure, pipeline freshness, retrieval health, model-serving errors,

00:00:15.113 --> 00:00:17.142
dependency failures, and policy decisions.

00:00:17.142 --> 00:00:21.606
Alert design should prioritize user impact, operational risk, and clear ownership.

00:00:21.606 --> 00:00:25.257
Service-level objectives help teams define what good behavior means.

00:00:25.257 --> 00:00:30.532
For AI systems, SLOs may cover inference latency, availability, error rates, timeout rates,

00:00:30.532 --> 00:00:34.184
queue delay, retrieval availability, pipeline freshness, model-serving success rates,

00:00:34.184 --> 00:00:35.807
and data processing reliability.

00:00:35.807 --> 00:00:42.300
These objectives turn vague concerns into measurable expectations that engineering and operations teams can discuss together.

00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:44.328
Runbooks connect alerts to action.

00:00:44.328 --> 00:00:49.198
A useful runbook points responders toward likely causes, dashboards, logs, traces, owners,

00:00:49.198 --> 00:00:51.632
escalation paths, and safe remediation steps.

00:00:51.632 --> 00:00:56.501
It should explain how to distinguish a model-serving issue from scheduler pressure,

00:00:56.501 --> 00:01:00.965
stale retrieval data, network delay, storage bottlenecks, or an external dependency.

00:01:00.965 --> 00:01:06.240
Runbooks should also clarify what not to do when the evidence is incomplete.

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:09.486
Incident-ready telemetry preserves evidence about impact, timeline, scope,

00:01:09.486 --> 00:01:10.703
and contributing factors.

00:01:10.703 --> 00:01:16.384
After an incident, the review should improve telemetry, not just fix the immediate issue.

00:01:16.384 --> 00:01:21.659
If responders lacked a label, dashboard, audit record, freshness signal, or correlation ID,

00:01:21.659 --> 00:01:24.499
that gap is part of the learning.

00:01:24.499 --> 00:01:27.745
Better telemetry makes the next incident shorter, clearer,

00:01:27.745 --> 00:01:30.180
and less dependent on individual memory.
